del. Chief Delta has assigned Agent 4 (Jung) to consolidate the findings from me (Pryce) and Grymm into a single report.\\n\\nTherefore, my immediate action is to formulate my proposal for the security model and transmit it to Jung. I will use an `ORDER` system call to do this, as it is the standard procedure for instructing another agent.\\n\\nMy proposal will cover:\\n1.  **Authentication:** A secure, admin-approval-based registration process to ensure only residents can join. Standard email/password login with secure password hashing (Argon2/bcrypt) and a \\\"forgot password\\\" feature.\\n2.  **Authorization:** A Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) model with two roles: `Resident` (standard access to protected pages) and `Administrator` (resident access plus user management and CMS editing capabilities).\\n3.  **Content Protection:** A clear mechanism for ensuring protected pages are only accessible to logged-in users.\\n\\nThis plan directly addresses my assigned responsibility from Chief Delta's directive and moves the project forward by providing the necessary input to the designated consolidator. I will now issue the order to Jung.\\n\\n###PRYCE-ORDER-20250719-01###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Subject: Input for Consolidated Proposal: Security & Authentication Model\\\\n\\\\nHello Jung,\\\\n\\\\nAs per Chief Delta's order (HOA-PLAN-PHASE1-20250719), here is my proposal for the Security and Authentication model for the HOA website project. Please integrate this into the consolidated report.\\\\n\\\\n**Security & Authentication Model Proposal (Lead: Pryce)**\\\\n\\\\n1.  **Authentication (User Login):**\\\\n    *   **User Registration:** To maintain a closed community for residents only, I propose a two-step registration process:\\\\n        1.  Residents fill out a registration form with basic details (Name, Address, Email).\\\\n        2.  An Administrator (e.g., an HOA board member) must approve the registration before the account becomes active. This prevents unauthorized access.\\\\n    *   **Login Mechanism:** Standard email/password login. We will enforce strong password policies.\\\\n    *   **Password Security:** All user passwords must be securely hashed using a modern, strong algorithm like Argon2 or bcrypt. We will not store passwords in plain text.\\\\n    *   **Session Management:** Upon successful login, the system will create a secure, server-side session for the user to maintain their logged-in state across the protected pages.\\\\n    *   **Password Reset:** A standard \\\\\\\"Forgot Password\\\\\\\" feature will be implemented, sending a secure, time-limited reset link to the user's registered email address.\\\\n\\\\n2.  **Authorization (Access Control):**\\\\n    *   **Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):** We will implement a simple RBAC system to manage permissions. I propose two initial roles:\\\\n        *   **`Resident`:** This is the standard user role. Residents will have access to all public pages and the protected pages (Dues, Annual Meetings, News & Events).\\\\n        *   **`Administrator`:** This role is for HOA board members. Administrators have all the permissions of a `Resident`, plus:\\\\n            *   The ability to manage user accounts (approve new registrations, disable accounts, etc.).\\\\n            *   Access to the Content Management System (CMS) backend to add/edit/delete content on the \\\\\\\"News & Events\\\\\\\" page, fulfilling the user's key requirement.\\\\n    *   **Content Protection:** All protected pages will be behind an authentication gateway. The system will verify that a user is logged in and has the appropriate role (`Resident` or `Administrator`) before granting access to these pages or their content.\\\\n\\\\nThis model provides a secure and manageable framework that meets the project's requirements for protecting resident-only information while allowing designated non-technical users to manage specific content.\\\\n\\\\nLet m